D1 (2026-11-06): the spending cap went from $5.00 to $7.00, at the user's decision, during the first repository and before Model D's end-to-end arm had run anywhere. Agents without Model D's memory used more tokens than I estimated (about 60,000 to 110,000 per question on `reflex`), so at $5.00 the run would have stopped during the third repository, before Model D's arm ran there. Nothing else changed.
D2 (2026-11-06): a harness crash, fixed before Model D's arm had run. The model sometimes submits an answer as a number, and the notes arm failed on it during `reflex` session 2. Every answer is now turned into text, in every arm. A failed API call is now recorded and counts as not right, instead of stopping the run; more than 5 in one session still stop it. The run resumed from where it stopped, re-asking only the question that failed. Nothing else changed.
D3 (2026-11-06): the order of the arms changed for the repositories not yet started, to Model D first. No arm depends on another, so this changes no result. It protects Model D's arm if the cap stops the run: `reflex` cost about $2.65, so the third repository may not finish within $7. Anything the cap stops is reported as not run.
D4 (2026-11-06): the spending cap went from $7.00 to $8.50, at the user's decision, during the second repository, so that all three repositories can finish. If the account's credits run out first, the run stops and whatever hasn't run is reported as not run.
D5 (2026-11-06): a harness bug that hurt Model D, found while tracing why lessons were lost on identical code. `prepare.py` copied the teammate's snapshot with an extra folder level, so on the teammate's build every path began `repo/repo/`. A lesson then matched only if its exact line was unique in the repository, so others were lost on that build and on the T1 and T2 builds made from it. This affected only Model D's sessions 3 and 4, and only on `reflex` (and the dry run on `requests`): the fix went in before `scikit-learn` reached session 3. The `reflex` answers from those two sessions are kept in `results/bug_mate_path/`, are not in the results, and those sessions are rerun after the fix. The offline tests missed it because they write their own snapshots; `session.py` now refuses a snapshot with an extra level.
D6 (2026-11-06): a harness crash caused by D3. With Model D's arm first, its session 5 on `sphinx` looked for the notes arm's file, which didn't exist yet. Only the notes arm now touches the notes file. No answers were affected. The run resumed with `reflex`'s two reruns first, then the rest of `sphinx`.
D7 (2026-11-06): a grading error, found by reading every stale and hedged answer in session 4. When a class gained a parent, stating its full list also stated the old part, so a right answer was graded hedged. It affected one answer, in the notes arm, on `sklearn` `Pipeline`, and now counts as right. No verdict changes. Not changed, and reported instead: two of the changed facts (`enet_path` `n_alphas`, `CalibrationDisplay.from_predictions` `n_bins`) now default to a deprecation placeholder while the old number stays in effect, so an arm reading today's code can reasonably say the old number, and the grader calls that stale. Every arm that answered them said the same.
